Chinese calendar: match the Hong Kong Observatory / Purple Mountain Observatory
tables for 1901-2100. ICU's CalendarAstronomer puts nine new moons and three
principal solar terms on the wrong side of local midnight (UTC+8), which moves
a month start by a day (CNY 1954, CNY 2027, CNY 2030, 1955-02, 1999-01,
2012-08, 2018-11, 2070-03, 2101-06) or moves a leap month one lunation late
(1917, 1922, 1987). Apple's ICU and ICU4X (V8) already ship these dates.

Based on the chnsecal.cpp part of https://github.com/unicode-org/icu/pull/4070
(ICU-23274, ICU-22230, ICU-23286), with two changes: the corrections only apply
to the Chinese calendar's astronomical zone (DangiCalendar shares this code and
the Korean tables agree with the uncorrected days), and a corrected new moon
keeps the before/after contract of the search, so a date on the computed day of
an early conjunction belongs to the previous month instead of getting day 0.
Verified per day for 1900-2101 (field consistency and round trips) and per
lunation against ICU4X's precomputed tables: chinese agrees everywhere except
2057-09-28/29 (left as computed, the conjunction is 40 s from midnight and the
sources disagree), dangi is unchanged. Drop once ICU ships ICU-23274.

